(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 6-year-old with known atopic dermatitis develops painful, uniform punched-out erosions over a flaring area, with fever and malaise. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin a topical calcineurin inhibitor","Order patch testing","Order herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction","Increase the potency of the topical corticosteroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Painful monomorphic erosions with systemic illness is the stated trigger for this test, since eczema herpeticum needs excluding before treatment escalates. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with long-standing venous insufficiency has itchy, hyperpigmented plaques over both gaiter regions with induration. His ankle-brachial index is normal. Which is the most appropriate management?","choices":["High-potency topical corticosteroids as the sole long-term therapy","Strict bed rest with the legs dependent","Compression therapy, with leg elevation, walking and calf-muscle exercises","Long-term systemic antibiotics"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — compression is the cornerstone once arterial circulation is adequate. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 117"},{"q":"A 41-year-old intensive care nurse reports several weeks of worsening dryness, redness and scaling of both hands, worst over the dorsal surfaces. She washes her hands more than thirty times a shift. The rash is well demarcated with a glazed appearance and there are no vesicles. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dyshidrotic eczema","Psoriasis","Irritant contact dermatitis","Allergic contact dermatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the commonest form of contact dermatitis, frequently occupational, with soaps and detergents as classic irritants and a glazed well-demarcated appearance. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a sudden shower of small raindrop-shaped scaly pink papules over the trunk and proximal limbs. He had a sore throat about two weeks ago. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Nummular eczema","Guttate psoriasis","Pityriasis rosea","Plaque psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — raindrop-shaped papules in a child 2 to 3 weeks after streptococcal infection or an upper respiratory infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"A 26-year-old developed an itchy rash confined to a rectangle on her forearm, sparing a small central area, three days after a minor injury. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Future exposure to the same adhesive will not provoke a reaction once this episode has settled","The reaction is to the adhesive in the dressing, and the spared centre corresponds to the cotton pad","The rash is contagious and she should avoid close contact until it clears","The reaction indicates the wound is infected and needs antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the shape reproducing the object is the hallmark of allergic contact dermatitis to adhesives. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 4-month-old infant has weeping inflammatory patches and crusted plaques on the cheeks, scalp and extensor surfaces of the arms. The mother reports the baby rubs against bedding rather than scratching. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Nummular eczema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The infantile pattern favours cheeks, scalp and extensor surfaces, and rubbing replaces scratching at this age. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has an oval scaly plaque on his chest that appeared ten days ago, and now smaller oval lesions are spreading down his trunk. Which is the most appropriate initial diagnostic approach?","choices":["Clinical diagnosis based on the herald patch and the typical pattern","Skin biopsy of the herald patch","Potassium hydroxide preparation taken from the herald patch","Serum rapid plasma reagin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the herald patch and cleavage-line distribution are enough. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has a glazed, well-demarcated dermatitis over both eyelids that began after she started using a new cosmetic. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis","Atopic dermatitis","Seborrhoeic dermatitis","Perioral dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the eyelids and face are among the commonest sites, from makeup and masks, and the glazed well-demarcated appearance fits. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has recurrent tapioca-like vesicles on his palms confirmed clinically as dyshidrotic eczema. Which is the most appropriate first-line treatment?","choices":["Oral prednisone tapered over three weeks","A high-potency topical corticosteroid","A low-potency topical corticosteroid","A topical antifungal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. High potency is specified first line here, with systemic corticosteroids reserved for severe cases. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman has diffuse thinning over the central and parietal scalp with her frontal hairline preserved. Which is the most appropriate treatment consideration?","choices":["Antifungal shampoo","Oral antiandrogens such as combined oral contraceptives may be considered, since treatment options are limited","Oral finasteride prescribed as first-line therapy for female-pattern hair loss at any age after puberty","Intralesional corticosteroids"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — female-pattern hair loss has fewer options, and finasteride is not indicated in women. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman with rapidly progressive alopecia areata is distressed and withdrawing socially. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Psychological support is one of the most important considerations, and support groups may be offered","The hair loss is permanent, because the follicle and its stem cells have been destroyed","The condition is contagious to household members","Nail changes exclude the diagnosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is named among the most important factors in managing this condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has extensive perioral dermatitis that has persisted despite stopping her topical corticosteroid and simplifying her routine. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Topical high-potency corticosteroid","Oral tetracycline or doxycycline","Topical metronidazole","Oral prednisone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — more extensive or persistent disease may require an oral tetracycline. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has a purulent wound and the clinician wants to know both what is growing and what will treat it. Which test should be ordered?","choices":["Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Tzanck smear of the wound base","Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Fungal culture of the wound surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the culture names the organism and the sensitivity directs the antibiotic. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 10-month-old has a diaper rash involving the inguinal folds with satellite papules beyond the main area. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Skin biopsy","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Patch testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — fold involvement and satellite lesions suggest Candida, and identification of budding yeast or pseudohyphae supports it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has patchy hair loss. Dermoscopy shows yellow dots, black dots, broken hairs, tapered hairs and short regrowth. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Androgenetic alopecia","Trichotillomania","Tinea capitis","Alopecia areata"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is usually clinical and is strengthened by exactly this dermoscopic pattern. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 139"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with widespread flaccid bullae and painful oral erosions has a biopsy showing acantholysis. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Observation, since the disease usually remits in five to six years","Oral doxycycline as monotherapy","Rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone, given urgently","Ultrapotent topical steroids alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — pemphigus is life-threatening, so treatment is urgent, and a steroid-sparing agent is usually added so steroids can be tapered. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has suspected lichen planus. Which biopsy finding confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Acantholysis","Neutrophils aligned in a row at the dermal-epidermal junction","Munro microabscesses within the stratum corneum","Band-like infiltration of lymphocytes in the dermis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with hyperkeratosis without parakeratosis, basal vacuolisation, Civatte bodies and a saw-tooth rete ridge. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 177"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has erosive oral and vulvar lichen planus. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Mucosal involvement resolves spontaneously and needs no follow-up","The condition is contagious to sexual partners","Hepatitis C is the established cause of this condition, so treating the infection will reliably clear the rash","Genital and erosive oral disease carry an increased risk of squamous cell carcinoma, so ongoing surveillance is needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — this is the reason mucosal involvement must be identified and followed. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"A 22-year-old presents two days after clearing brush in the garden with intensely itchy blisters in streaks across both forearms. Examination shows linear vesicles and papules in several stages, with excoriations. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Irritant contact dermatitis of the hands","Herpes zoster","Bullous pemphigoid","Allergic contact dermatitis from urushiol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The linear distribution comes from grazing the plant in passing, and lesions erupt 4 to 96 hours after exposure. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with recurrent palmar vesicles asks how to reduce flares. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["Wash the hands frequently with an antibacterial soap through the course of the day, in order to prevent secondary bacterial infection of the ruptured vesicles","Use hot water to relieve the itching","Apply emollients only when the skin feels dry","Avoid irritants such as detergents, solvents and hair dyes, wash with lukewarm water and soap-free cleanser, dry hands thoroughly, and apply emollients immediately afterwards"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — trigger avoidance and barrier care are the named education points. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"A 46-year-old with known plaque psoriasis stops a course of oral prednisone prescribed elsewhere. Days later she develops fever for several days, then an abrupt generalised eruption of 2 to 3 mm pustules over the trunk and limbs including the palms and nail beds, some coalescing into lakes of pus. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Begin oral antibiotics for a presumed bacterial pustular infection","Restart the oral prednisone at the previous dose and then continue it indefinitely thereafter","Reassure and review in two weeks, since psoriasis flares are expected","Recognise this as pustular psoriasis of the Von Zumbusch type and treat it as a life-threatening emergency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it is abrupt and life-threatening, may follow systemic steroid withdrawal, and is commonly mistaken for bacterial or viral infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 160"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man develops an itchy violaceous papular eruption on his wrists two months after starting a new antihypertensive. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Test for hepatitis C and treat it to clear the rash","Reassure that the eruption is self-limited and requires no action","Review the medication list, since several drugs produce lichenoid reactions","Begin superpotent topical steroids without further history"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, sulfonamides, tetracyclines, hydrochlorothiazide, quinidine and some beta blockers are named. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 173"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman has had several weeks of intense itching with urticarial plaques but no blisters. She is otherwise well. Which is the most likely explanation?","choices":["The prodromal phase of bullous pemphigoid, which may precede bullae by weeks to months","Chronic idiopathic urticaria entirely unrelated to any blistering disease","Early pemphigus vulgaris","Lichen simplex chronicus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the prodrome presents as pruritic urticarial or oedematous lesions before the blistering phase. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man has several round scaly plaques on his forearms. The lesions are uniform throughout without central clearing, and he has been applying an over-the-counter antifungal for three weeks without improvement. Which is the best initial diagnostic test?","choices":["Punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence","Serum immunoglobulin E level","Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Potassium hydroxide preparation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis is the main differential, and it must be excluded before committing to a diagnosis of nummular eczema. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has several intensely itchy round plaques on his lower legs, 2 to 4 cm across, light pink with scale and crusting. The plaques are uniform throughout without any central clearing. Which is the most appropriate initial diagnostic test?","choices":["Allergy patch testing","Punch biopsy for direct immunofluorescence","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Bacterial culture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tinea corporis is the main differential and is excluded by potassium hydroxide, though the absent central clearing already argues against it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has thick psoriatic plaques on both elbows that have not responded to a moderate potency steroid. Which agent from the potency table is the highest available?","choices":["Triamcinolone acetonide 0.1%","Clobetasol propionate 0.05%","Betamethasone valerate 0.1%","Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it is the only agent in the high potency tier on this table. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man with deeply pigmented skin has a warm, tender, swollen forearm. The examiner cannot appreciate any erythema. Which is the most appropriate approach?","choices":["Rely on palpation, distribution and secondary change, since erythema is an unreliable signal on darker skin","Conclude that inflammation is absent, since visible erythema is required in order to diagnose it","Perform a Wood's lamp examination to bring out the erythema in a dark room","Take a punch biopsy immediately, since clinical assessment cannot be relied on"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the finding is present but the colour cue is not. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 114"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman has adult-onset eczema that has not responded to appropriate topical therapy over several months. Her rash involves the eyelids and hands. Which is the most appropriate diagnostic test?","choices":["Patch testing","Potassium hydroxide preparation","Skin-prick testing","Tzanck smear"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — patch testing is indicated for atypical, adult-onset or treatment-resistant disease, and eyelid and hand involvement raises contact allergy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman has widespread tense bullae and a clinical suspicion of bullous pemphigoid. Which test confirms the diagnosis?","choices":["Biopsy of perilesional tissue for direct immunofluorescence","Potassium hydroxide preparation of the blister roof","Tzanck smear of a fresh blister","Bacterial culture of blister fluid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lesional tissue goes for histopathology and perilesional tissue for immunofluorescence, with serum studies or ELISA identifying anti-basement membrane zone antibodies. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with recurrent greasy scaling of the scalp and nasolabial folds asks how long treatment will be needed. Which is the most appropriate counselling point?","choices":["The condition is chronic and relapsing, so repeated and long-term use of medication is often required","A single two-week course of antifungal shampoo is usually curative and needs no repeating","It will resolve permanently once stress is controlled","It is contagious and household members should be treated as well"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that expectation is stated explicitly for this condition. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 95"}]};
